  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products [10-K line 00134]. The company's business model relies on high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover, achieved through volume purchasing, efficient distribution, and reduced handling in no-frills, self-service warehouse facilities [10-K line 00134].\n\nCostco's strategy is to provide members with a broad range of high-quality merchandise at consistently lower prices than elsewhere [10-K line 00141]. The company operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites worldwide [10-K line 00131]. Membership fees are $65 annually in the U.S., varying by country, and include a free household card [10-K line 00157].\n\nThe company's warehouse operations feature strict control over entrances and exits to minimize inventory losses (shrinkage) [10-K line 00139]. Costco typically carries fewer than 4,000 SKUs per warehouse in its core business, significantly less than other broadline retailers [10-K line 00141].\n\nCostco also offers various ancillary services such as gasoline stations, pharmacy, food courts, optical services, and tire installation [10-K line 00148]. Additionally, the company engages in e-commerce, business centers, travel, and other businesses that complement its warehouse operations [10-K line 00153].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes with global, national, regional wholesalers and retailers including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and operators selling narrow ranges of merchandise [10-K line 00190]. In the U.S., significant competitors include Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon [10-K line 00190]. Other warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club also compete with Costco in the U.S. [10-K line 00190].\n\nThe company's competitive advantages include its membership model, which reinforces member loyalty and provides continuing fee revenue [10-K line 00406]. Costco differentiates itself through private-label products such as Kirkland Signature, which are high quality, offered at prices generally lower than national brands, and help lower costs while differentiating offerings [10-K line 00192].\n\n## Catalysts\nKey catalysts for Costco include membership growth, particularly Executive memberships that earn rewards redeemable at warehouses [10-K line 00166]. A high member renewal rate (92.3% in U.S. and Canada, 89.8% globally) supports profitability [10-K line 00167]. The company's ability to leverage SG&A expenses through increased comparable sales enhances profitability [10-K line 00398].\n\nCostco's expansion strategy includes opening new warehouses, which drives net sales growth [10-K line 00405]. The company opened 28 new warehouses in 2026, including relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses [release line 00014].\n\nDigital-enabled sales have grown significantly, representing 11% of total net sales in 2026 and increasing by 21% during 2026 [10-K line 00452]. The company's e-commerce operations ship merchandise through depots, logistics operations, and drop-ship arrangements with suppliers [10-K line 00139].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn 2026, Costco reported a 10% increase in net sales to $297.2 billion, driven by comparable sales growth and sales from new warehouses [10-K line 00451]. Comparable sales increased 8%, with average ticket increases of 5% and shopping frequency increases of 3% [10-K line 00451].\n\nDigitally-enabled comparable sales increased 21% in 2026 [10-K line 00452]. Higher gasoline prices positively impacted net sales by $3.5 billion, or 130 basis points, with a 12% increase in average price per gallon and volume growth of 7% [10-K line 00454].\n\nMembership fee revenue increased 11% to $5.9 billion, driven by new member sign-ups, membership fee increases, and upgrades to Executive memberships [10-K line 00458]. At the end of 2026, member renewal rates were 92.3% in the U.S. and Canada and 89.8% worldwide [10-K line 00459].\n\nGross margin percentage decreased three basis points to 11.09%, but increased 10 basis points excluding the impact of gasoline price inflation [10-K line 00469]. SG&A expenses as a percentage of net sales decreased 10 basis points, increasing one basis point excluding the impact of gasoline price inflation [10-K line 00476].\n\nCostco's net income increased 14% to $9.2 billion, or $20.76 per diluted share, compared to $8.1 billion, or $18.21 per diluted share in 2025 [10-K line 00422]. In April 2026, the Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [10-K line 00423].\n\nThe company received $184 million in IEEPA tariff refunds during 2026, with $174 recorded as a reduction to merchandise costs and $10 as interest income [10-K line 00426]. These refunds were partially reinvested in member price reductions [10-K line 00426].\n\nCostco opened 28 new warehouses in 2026, including three relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses [release line 00014]. The company plans to open up to 33 new warehouses in 2027, including five relocations [10-K line 00513].\n\nCostco reported strong cash flow from operations, with $15.8 billion in net cash provided by operating activities compared to $13.3 billion in 2025 [10-K line 00509]. The company's cash and cash equivalents increased to $20.2 billion at the end of 2026 from $14.2 billion at the end of 2025 [release line 00067].",
